// XFAIL: string interpolation is not feature-complete.
//
// One consolidated regression test for every open interpolation defect. It
// does not compile today. Each numbered section below names the bug it covers
// and what its expected output line proves. When all of them are fixed this
// test XPASSes and should be promoted to tests/pass/.
//
// [1] Facets are pointer-only, so no primitive and no temporary can ever be a
//     hole. satisfyFacet (src/zsem.c) opens with
//       if (type->kind != Z_TYPE_POINTER) return false;
//     and an impl block on a value receiver is rejected outright with
//       Z303C: Facets must be implemented only for pointer types
//     Both holes in the design doc's own example -- "hello from {name}, i'm
//     {age} years old" -- are unreachable because of this; only {expr.&} on a
//     struct pointer works. Sections 3 and 4 cover the two sub-cases: a named
//     value, and an rvalue, which additionally needs an implicit stack temp to
//     have an address at all.
//
// [2] `{{` / `}}` escapes are not implemented, though
//     docs/string-interpolation.md section 4.5 says they are. `#"a{{b}}c"`
//     currently tries to resolve `b` as an identifier -> Z3014. There is at
//     present no way to write a literal `{` in an interpolated string.
//     Section 5.
//
// [3] A plain string literal does not coerce to []InterpolatedString, so
//       print("done\n")
//     fails with Z0009: Expected '[]enum InterpolatedString', got '[6]char'.
//     Every call site is forced to spell the `#`, which makes `print` a
//     strictly worse printf. Section 6.
//
// [4] Empty literal runs between adjacent holes are emitted rather than
//     dropped (docs section 4.3 says they are dropped). `#"{a}{b}"` lowers to
//     four entries -- Lit(len 0), Obj, Lit(len 0), Obj -- costing two
//     `[1 x i8] zeroinitializer` globals and two zero-length write() calls at
//     run time. Section 7 counts the writes: 4 today, 2 once fixed.
//
// [5] Enum payload layout is wrong, and interpolation is what makes it
//     load-bearing -- it is the first feature to put payload enums in arrays
//     as core machinery. genEnumType (src/codegen/zgen.c) sets the body to
//     { flag, [typeSize - 1 x i8] }, but typeSize measures the variant types,
//     which already carry their own tag, so the tag is counted twice. The
//     [N x i8] buffer also gives the enum align 1 while every payload access
//     GEPs through an align-8 variant type:
//       %InterpolatedString = type { i8, [24 x i8] }    ; size 25, align 1
//       %Literal            = type { i8, { i64, ptr } } ; payload at +8, align 8
//     So the array stride is 25, element 1's payload lands at byte 33, and it
//     is stored with an 8-byte store declared `align 8` at an address that is
//     1 mod 8. It survives on arm64 today; it is UB the optimizer is free to
//     act on. The correct size is tag(1) + pad(7) + payload(16) = 24.
//     Section 8. Reproduces without interpolation:
//       E :: enum { A(i64) B(*char) }
//     gives %E = { i8, [16 x i8] } against %A = { i8, i64 }.
//
// Two further defects cannot live in this file:
//   - Errors raised inside a hole report `:0:0` with no line, column or source
//     snippet, because the re-lexed sub-token stream built by
//     parseInterpolatedString (src/zlex.c) drops positions. Asserting that
//     needs stderr matching; run_tests.sh only compares the binary's stdout.
//   - Writer.write returns void, so I/O errors and byte counts are discarded,
//     and Obj(Writable) has no FmtSpec field, so `{age:x}` cannot be added
//     without breaking every impl. Both are API shape, not behaviour.
//
// Fixed since first triage, kept below as regression cover: a hole whose type
// has no `sink` used to compile silently and produce a binary that crashed on
// a garbage vtable, and `#"a{}b"` used to SIGSEGV the compiler in resolveType.
//
// The two `sink` impls below are written against `sink : fn (Writer) void`. They
// track std.lib's Writable facet, so if FmtSpec lands they each need the
// second parameter. Nothing else here depends on that signature.

use <std.lib>

printf :: foreign fn (*char, ..) void

// A sink that echoes to stdout and counts how many write() calls the lowering
// actually produced, so section 7 can observe empty literal runs.
Probe :: struct {
    writes: i32
}

*Probe self :: impl(Writer) {
    write :: fn (buff: *u8, size: usize) {
        self.writes = self.writes + 1
        printf("%.*s", size as i32, buff as *char)
    }
}

User :: struct {
    id: i32
}

*User self :: impl(Writable) {
    sink :: fn (w: Writer) {
        w.write("U" as *u8, 1)
    }
}

// Bug [1]: rejected today with Z303C. A hole is an ordinary expression, so
// every type that can appear in one needs a Writable impl -- and the types
// that appear in practice are values, not pointers.
i32 self :: impl(Writable) {
    sink :: fn (w: Writer) {
        d := (48 + self) as char
        w.write(d.& as *u8, 1)
    }
}

main :: fn () i32 {
    p   := Probe{writes=0}
    w   := Writer(p.&)
    u   := User{id=1}
    age := 7

    // 1. Literal-only interpolation. Works today; regression cover.
    interpolate(w, #"one\n")

    // 2. Literal / hole alternation through a pointer receiver. Works today;
    //    this is currently the only shape of hole the compiler accepts.
    interpolate(w, #"a{u.&}b\n")

    // 3. Bug [1]: a named value as a hole. `age` is an i32, not a pointer, so
    //    satisfyFacet rejects it no matter what impls exist.
    interpolate(w, #"age={age}\n")

    // 4. Bug [1]: an rvalue as a hole. Beyond auto-ref, this needs the
    //    lowering to materialise a stack temp -- `1 + 2` has no address.
    interpolate(w, #"sum={1 + 2}\n")

    // 5. Bug [2]: `{{` and `}}` are escapes and stay inside the literal run,
    //    so this is one literal and prints a{b}c.
    interpolate(w, #"a{{b}}c\n")

    // 6. Bug [3]: a plain literal is a one-element interpolation.
    interpolate(w, "plain\n")

    // 7. Bug [4]: two adjacent holes, no literal text between or around them.
    //    That is exactly two writes. Today the lowering emits a zero-length
    //    Literal before each hole and reports 4.
    p2 := Probe{writes=0}
    interpolate(Writer(p2.&), #"{u.&}{u.&}")
    printf("\nadjacent writes=%d\n", p2.writes)

    // 8. Bug [5]: tag(1) + padding(7) + largest payload(16) = 24.
    //    Today 25, because the variant's own tag is counted a second time and
    //    the payload is a byte array with no alignment of its own.
    printf("sizeof=%d\n", sizeof InterpolatedString)

    return 0
}
